<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huldrych Zwingli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1484">1484</a>–<a href="/years/1531">1531</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ulrich Zwingli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huldrych Zwingli was a Swiss priest and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in Zürich. After serving as parish priest at Glarus, he moved to Zürich in 1519, where his preaching led the city to abolish the Mass in 1525. His teachings shaped Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. He died as a chaplain with the Zürich army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kappel">Battle of Kappel</a> in 1531.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli.jpg" alt="Huldrych Zwingli" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Asper · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulrich-Zwingli-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Huldrych Zwingli</b>, people’s priest at the Grossmünster from 1519, led the first Reformation in the Swiss Confederacy: after the public disputation of January 1523 the council adopted his programme, images were removed from the churches and in 1525 the Mass was replaced by a simple communion service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Huldrych Zwingli</b> began reform in Zürich from 1519, and the allied city of Geneva, where John Calvin worked from 1536 and founded his academy in 1559, became the centre of Reformed Protestantism, whose ideas spread to France, the Netherlands, Scotland, Hungary and the English-speaking world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Zurich <b>Huldrych Zwingli</b> taught that the Lord’s Supper was a memorial of Christ’s sacrifice, while Luther insisted that Christ’s body and blood were truly present in the bread and wine; at the Marburg Colloquy of 1529 they agreed on fourteen articles of faith but not on this…</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Martin Luther translated the New Testament into German from Erasmus’s Greek text, and <b>Huldrych Zwingli</b>, Melanchthon and John Calvin were humanists by training, as were many Catholic reformers.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Kappel am Albis, the Catholic cantons of central Switzerland defeated Protestant Zürich, and the reformer <b>Huldrych Zwingli</b>, who accompanied the Zürich army as chaplain, was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-11">11 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On 11 October they defeated Zürich at Kappel, where the reformer <b>Huldrych Zwingli</b> was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wildhaus, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Died in Kappel am Albis, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.2,9.35&amp;year=1514" aria-label="Wildhaus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4769 507 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Huldrych Zwingli"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5219 777h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5219 777h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.2,9.35&amp;year=1514">What was here in 1514? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
